[s0l0!-header][h1]The switch Statement[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The [b]switch [/b]statement tests a variable against a list of values, which are called [b]cases[/b], to determine whether it is equal to any of them.[code format="cpp" ][b]switch [/b](expression) {
  [b]case [/b]value1:
    statement(s);
   [b] break;[/b]
  [b]case [/b]value2:
    statement(s);
   [b] break;[/b]
    ...
  [b]case [/b]valueN:
    statement(s);
    [b]break;[/b]
}[/code]
Switch evaluates the expression to determine whether it's equal to the value in the case statement. If a match is found, it executes the statements in that case.
[note]A switch can contain any number of [b]case [/b]statements, which are followed by the [b]value [/b]in question and a [b]colon[/b].[/note]

