[s0l0!-header][h1]The break Statement[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The [b]break [/b]statement's role is to terminate the switch statement.
In instances in which the variable is equal to a case, the statements that come after the case continue to execute until they encounter a [b]break [/b]statement. In other words, leaving out a [b]break [/b]statement results in the execution of all of the statements in the following cases, even those that don't match the expression.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="cpp" codeId="212"]int age = 42;
switch (age) {
  case 16:
    cout << "Too young" << endl;
  case 42:
    cout << "Adult" << endl;
  case 70:
    cout << "Senior" << endl;
  default:
    cout <<"This is the default case" << endl;
}
[/code]
As you can see, the program executed the matching case statement, printing "Adult" to the screen. With no specified [b]break [/b]statement, the statements continued to run after the matching case. Thus, all the other case statements printed. This type of behavior is called [b]fall-through[/b].
[note]As the switch statement's final case, the [b]default [/b]case requires no [b]break [/b]statement.
The [b]break [/b]statement can also be used to break out of a loop.[/note]

